<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
Session identifiers must be unguessable. If a session identifier is built from a sequential
counter, possibly combined with a constant prefix, an attacker who observes a single valid
identifier (for example their own) can predict the identifiers issued to other users. The
attacker can then hijack those sessions or fix a victim's session to a known value.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Generate session identifiers with a cryptographically secure random number generator such as
<code>SecureRandom</code>, or rely on the session management provided by your web framework.
Do not derive session identifiers from counters, timestamps, or other predictable values.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
The following Sinatra route sets a session cookie whose value is a constant prefix followed by
a process-wide request counter. Every new identifier is the previous identifier plus one.
</p>
<sample language="ruby">
get "/token" do
  response.set_cookie("SessionID", "34A7EF-115C24-8F21CD-#{increment_count}")
end
</sample>
<p>
The fix is to generate the identifier from a secure random source:
</p>
<sample language="ruby">
get "/token" do
  response.set_cookie("SessionID", SecureRandom.hex(32))
end
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>MITRE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/384.html">CWE-384: Session Fixation</a>.</li>
<li>MITRE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html">CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values</a>.</li>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html">Session Management Cheat Sheet</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
